{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787688706,"nonce":"JAaLj8sJRR7hyB88VVB6wyMm0YY=","message":"RCA\n===\n\nThe reply buffer opened by 'r' in the review TUI starts with a contiguous\nblock of instruction lines, built in\nsrc/b4/review/_review.py:2023-2032 (_render_quoted_diff_with_comments):\n\n    result: List[str] = [\n        '# Insert your comments under the relevant lines of code. Lines',\n        ...\n        '#',\n    ]\n\nThat block is the only b4-owned # scaffolding, and it is always the leading\nrun of the buffer -- nothing below it is generated with a # prefix. But\nevery consumer filters # positionally-blind, over the whole buffer.\n\n1. Data loss on send (the reported symptom)\n-------------------------------------------\n\nsrc/b4/review/_review.py:3371, in _trim_quoted_reply(), which is the\nsend-time preparation of the buffer:\n\n    lines = [line for line in buffer.splitlines() if not line.startswith(('#', '|'))]\n\nThis is an unanchored filter over the entire buffer. Jason's\n\n    #define arm_smmu_kdump_is_attach_deferred NULL\n\nsits in his own prose, hundreds of lines below the header, and is removed\nhere. The docstring on the same function states the narrower intent -- \"strip\nb4's own scaffolding -- the ``#`` instruction header\" -- so the implementation\nis wider than its documented contract. This is the load-bearing site: it is\nwhat makes the text disappear from the mail that actually goes out, and it\nsilently mangles the message rather than failing.\n\nIt also collides with the WYSIWYG contract the reply buffer is built on\n(_review_app.py:1372-1383: \"The edited buffer is the single source of truth:\nstore it verbatim and never mutate it here\"). The buffer is stored verbatim,\nso the TUI shows the maintainer their #define; only the sent mail lacks it.\nThe divergence is invisible until someone reads the archive.\n\n2. Display index built from the same blind filter\n-------------------------------------------------\n\nsrc/b4/review/_review.py:2118-2126, in _extract_editor_comments():\n\n    for line in edited_text.splitlines():\n        # Strip editor instruction lines\n        if line.startswith('#'):\n            continue\n\nCalled from _review_app.py:1392. Same unanchored pass. Consequence is\nnarrower -- these comments only drive code-viewer annotations and the trailer\noverlay -- but a comment whose text is a single #-prefixed line loses that\nline from its annotation, and a comment that is *only* such a line can vanish\nfrom the index entirely.\n\n3. Trailer helpers share the assumption\n---------------------------------------\n\nsrc/b4/review/_review.py:3268 (_parse_reply_trailers) and :3317\n(_remove_trailer_from_reply) both treat any #-prefixed line as scaffolding.\nEffects here are benign-to-protective: a trailer typed after a # is not\nindexed, and _remove_trailer_from_reply declines to touch such lines, which\nis the conservative direction. Listed for completeness because they encode\nthe same \"# means b4's\" premise and would drift from the other two if only\nthose were narrowed.\n\nRelated\n-------\n\nsrc/b4/review_tui/_review_app.py:1566-1569, the private-note editor, filters\n# over the whole note the same way. Distinct from the report -- notes are\nnever sent, and the footer there does say \"Lines starting with # will be\nremoved\" -- but a note containing a #define loses it identically, and the\nsame anchoring question applies.\n\nsrc/b4/review_tui/_tracking_app.py:569 and _review.py:549 (load_tracking) also\nstrip # lines, but those parse the JSON tracking blob, where # comments are\ngenuinely b4-generated. Not part of this bug.\n\nNot reproduced against a live thread; the paths above are read directly from\nthe source and the filters are unconditional list comprehensions, so the\nmechanism is proven by inspection rather than inferred.","files":null}]}